/** The 3 Nov audit rewrites (23:00 GMT pass), by post id. Each replaces the post text until its re-check. */
export const auditTexts: Record<string, string> = {
  d1: "When I wrote the deadlift counter, I ended up watching just one number: the hip angle. Stand up past 160°, hinge down past 125°, cover most of that range, and that's a rep.\n\nThe harder part is everything that looks like a rep and isn't, like jitter and half reps. So a rep can't be too quick, and one that finishes less than a second after the last one gets thrown out.\n\nThe joint points from the camera need calming down too. Each one goes through a One Euro filter, which smooths hard while you're still and backs off as you move, so the line doesn't lag behind you.\n\nAnd nobody hinges quite the same way. Once you've done one proper rep, the top line moves to fit you, sitting 12% of your own range below your highest reading.",
  d2: "Picking a pose model for a phone is a tradeoff, so I measured it. I ran Lite, Full and Heavy over 156 single-person photos from the COCO validation set.\n\nHeavy was the most accurate, but it would have added 21 MB to the app and ran about three times slower than Full. Full got 79% of joints close to the right spot, against 72% for Lite, for only a little more time a frame in my desktop test. So Full is the one the app ships.\n\nSpeed matters because the camera doesn't wait for you. If a frame is still in the model when a new one arrives, I skip the new one instead of queueing it, so the count follows what you're doing now. Anything that arrives late is thrown away instead of drawn.\n\nIf the phone still can't keep up after a short warm-up, the workout switches to Apple's Vision for the rest of that session. The next workout gives BlazePose another go.",
  d3: "Most of the work in counting reps with a phone camera, I've found, is not counting the things that aren't reps.\n\nSo nothing counts until the counter has seen the joints it needs on every frame for half a second. After that, a rep has to cover most of the distance between its two lines, can't be rushed, and can't land right on top of the last one.\n\nMore notes on the counter: https://ashishkharche.com/notes/",
  'wd-b2-2':
    "Sometimes an old build has to go. So every time Workout Director opens or comes back to the foreground, it asks the backend for the minimum iOS version, and any other request from a build below that minimum gets a 426 telling it to update.\n\nThe update screen has no close button, so it must never show to a build that's fine. The backend caches the minimum for 60 seconds, so I made the phone ignore any minimum at or below its own version. A stale cache can't lock out a current build.",
  'wd-b2-3':
    "The trainer chat in Workout Director looks simple: you type, it answers. Most of the work is in what goes into each request and what's allowed to come back.\n\nEvery request is built in the same order. What rarely changes goes first: the instructions, your profile, the facts the trainer has been told to remember and a summary of older chat. Then the recent messages, and only then what changes every turn, like today's plan and your recent training. Keeping the stable parts up front lets the prompt cache reuse them from one message to the next.\n\nLong chats get folded down. Once enough messages pile up, everything but the newest 24 goes into a short running summary.\n\nThe reply has a fixed shape too: a few replies, any memory changes, any food or training you mentioned, and, when you've just told it something about yourself, any change to your training profile. The prompt tells it to remember only lasting facts you actually stated, not moods or guesses.\n\nAnd if a flaky connection makes the phone send the same message twice, the backend spots the repeated id and returns the stored reply instead of asking the model again.",
  'wd-b2-4':
    "Every exercise figure in Workout Director is a render of the same 3D body. I didn't draw any of them by hand.\n\nThe body is MakeHuman's base mesh and rig. Each figure is posed in Blender to match joint angles measured from reference clips and photos, never traced. IK places the limbs, a grip solver closes the hands, and Blender's Freestyle renderer turns it all into line art.\n\nA pose model reads each reference, and its joint angles become the targets a figure is checked against. Other scripts catch feet under the floor or a bar that isn't level.\n\nSpeed mattered too. Switching the mesh modifiers off while the bones are solved took a bent-over row from about six minutes to under one.",
  'wd-b2-6':
    "This is one deadlift set the way the counter sees it: a woman lifting a barbell, filmed side-on, with her right hip angle drawn as a line. Going under the work line starts a rep, and coming back up past the rest line finishes it.\n\nBy eye she does three full reps and starts a fourth. The counter says 2, and each rep it leaves out has a reason.\n\nRep 1 comes out of a long set-up at the bottom. She never stood up straight around it, so the counter refuses it.\n\nReps 2 and 3 pass. The counter holds rep 2 until rep 3 finishes, then both count.\n\nRep 4 just isn't finished. She's still on her way up when the clip ends.",
  'wd-b3-1':
    "Every rep Workout Director counts comes from one joint angle crossing two lines. For a squat it's the knee, with lines at 155° and 110°. For a push-up it's the elbow.\n\nThose lines start out fixed in code, but people don't all move through the same range. So once you've done one rep of at least 15°, the start line moves to fit you, 12% of your range in from your own highest or lowest angle, depending on the move.\n\nYour range is saved per exercise and widens as you do more reps. I kept the line that finishes a rep from ever getting stricter than the default, and a rep still has to cover at least 20°, so a small twitch can't count.",
  'wd-b3-2':
    "At the bottom of a side-on deadlift, the far plate can hide the near ankle. The pose model gets less sure about where it is, and its confidence drops below what the counter normally needs.\n\nLosing a rep over one hidden ankle felt wrong when the hip and knee are in plain view. So if those two are still clear and the ankle hasn't faded too far, the counter keeps the leg in view for up to 3 s.\n\nIn the clip in the image, you can see the ankle dim at the bottom of the lift while the counter keeps going.",
  'wd-b3-4':
    'When the voice coach loses its connection, counting carries on, and the screen says "Coach reconnecting · counts still on".\n\nIn the background the phone keeps retrying, waiting longer each time, from 1 second up to 30, with a bit of random jitter on each wait. It stops once the next wait would take it past two and a half minutes.\n\nThe image shows those waits without the jitter. A slow network gets fewer tries, because the time each connection attempt takes comes out of the same two and a half minutes.',
  'wd-b3-6':
    "A single rep on its own is suspicious. It might just be someone bending down to tidy up between sets.\n\nSo for curls, presses, rows, squats and deadlifts, the counter holds the first rep for up to 8 seconds and waits for the next one. If it comes, both count. If none comes within 8 seconds, that lone bend is dropped and never counts. (End the set while it's still waiting and it does count, since one heavy rep can be a set.)\n\nOn curls, that took false reps from 35.4 an hour down to 3.4, at a cost of 2.0% of real reps.\n\nIn the clip in the image, rep 1 is refused for a different reason, so rep 2 is the first rep the counter accepts. It waits 5.48 s for rep 3, then both count.",
  'wd-b4-fig1':
    "Workout Director comes with data for 869 exercises, all from free-exercise-db, an open exercise dataset. But the app only offers the ones that have a figure, and right now that's 119.\n\nFour figures do double duty, so 115 figures cover those 119. Most show a start and an end pose. A few, like plank and farmer's walk, are a single pose.\n\nThe other 750 exercises aren't offered until they have a figure.",
  'wd-b4-p1':
    "Workout Director is an iPhone app for training at the gym or at home. You prop the phone up, press Start, and it counts your reps with the camera and tells you what's next.\n\nIt counts squats, lunges, push-ups, pull-ups, curls and rows, plus lifts like bench press, deadlifts and hip thrusts. Planks and holds are timed.\n\nThe camera reads your movement on the iPhone itself. You don't need to sign in for a camera workout.\n\nAround the counter there are training programs, a trainer that remembers your goals, equipment and schedule, and an optional voice coach. Every session is logged, and Apple Health is optional.\n\nFree on the App Store.",
  'wd-b4-tech1':
    "I didn't want a workout app that sends your camera feed off somewhere. So Workout Director counts reps from the phone camera, with pose tracking and counting running on the iPhone.\n\nThe pose model ships inside the app instead of being downloaded later. It's BlazePose Full, loaded from a file in the app bundle.\n\nIt runs on the GPU, falls back to the CPU, and after that to Apple's own on-device Vision pose estimator. All three paths run on the phone.",
  'wd-b5-1':
    "A plank has no reps, so the app times it instead. The hard part is deciding which seconds were actually a plank.\n\nThe clock only runs while your shoulder, hip and ankle make a near-straight line of at least 150°, with your body no more than 40° off horizontal. A short sag is forgiven, and so is a brief moment where tracking drops out.\n\nI tried it on a public plank clip labelled 9 seconds. The clock gives it 2. Most of that hold sits just under the straight line, and the test in my repo pins that 2 on purpose.\n\nI'd rather the clock be strict than flattering.",
  'wd-b5-2':
    "Push-ups often get filmed by a friend holding the phone, and the elbow angle the counter reads from that kind of footage is jumpy. One wobble can look like a dip.\n\nSo the counter takes the middle value of every 3 frames, needs the elbow to stay past the bottom line for 2 frames, and checks for blur, where even the slowest joint is moving too fast to trust.\n\nI tried it on a public kettlebell push-up clip, filmed on a tripod. The raw angle dips from the top line past the bottom line 8 times. With the filter it's 3, the same number my test expects. Five bottoms are labelled, but the arms only make it back to the top three times.",
  'wd-b5-3':
    "On 28 October the row counter counted me just setting up. It saw my elbow bend and decided that was a row.\n\nThe elbow angle on its own could be any arm move: walking up, lifting the bar, hinging over, even waving. So now rows only count when you're bent over and still, with your hips and knees in view.\n\nI checked it on a saved excerpt of a public row clip that keeps only the arm joints. The elbow swings through one rep played three times, but with no hip or knee in the data, the counter counts zero. That's the answer my test expects.",
  'wd-b5-4':
    'I\'m porting the rep counter from Swift to Kotlin. Watching a few sets and saying "looks right" wasn\'t going to be enough.\n\nSo iOS writes down what its counter outputs on every frame: the count, each rep, held seconds, tracking. The Kotlin test replays the same inputs and has to produce identical output, frame by frame.\n\nThere are 140 saved inputs, and 110 of them are synthetic: ten moves, each done eleven ways, including fast, slow, noisy, partly hidden and with a second person in frame.\n\nThe catch is that it matches the iOS counter as it was on 23 October. The newer posture, framing and two-rep checks, and other counter changes since then, still need porting.',
  'wd-b5-5':
    "A long set warms the phone up. So the counter checks how hot the phone says it is and paces the camera to match.\n\nNormally it processes 30 frames a second. When the phone reports it's running seriously hot, or Low Power Mode is on, it drops to 15. If it reaches critical, camera counting stops.\n\nThere's a separate safety net too. If the pose model can't start, or runs too slowly, that session falls back to Apple's own pose engine.\n\nI haven't measured what this does for battery. For now it's a rule in the code, not a result.",
  'wd-b5-6':
    "A bug I hit while porting to Kotlin: on the emulator, the pose model's GPU setup built without complaint, then failed on the very first frame with GL_INVALID_ENUM.\n\nMy code only fell back to the CPU when setup itself failed. Setup had worked, so nothing caught the failure, and pose detection just died.\n\nThe fix catches a frame that fails and switches to the CPU for the rest of the process. When it's reading a video, it runs that same frame again on the CPU; on the live camera it drops that frame and carries on from the next one.\n\nStarting up and actually working turned out to be two different checks.",
  'wd-b6-1':
    "A number on its own doesn't tell you much, so the targets on the Today screen come with one plain line explaining them.\n\nIf you finished all your sets last time, it says so and asks for a bit more, like one more rep or one more set. If the last session was hard, or you started it sore, the target stays the same.\n\nIf you didn't finish, it aims for your last full session. A lighter week and a new 8-week block get their own line too.\n\nThe line is worked out from your logged sessions with fixed rules, so it only says what the data shows. When there's nothing true to say, it says nothing.\n\nFree on the App Store.",
  'wd-b6-2':
    'In the gym it\'s an RDL, not a Romanian deadlift. The exercise search didn\'t know most of those names, so searching for what people actually call a move could come up empty.\n\nNow search knows the other names too. RDL finds the Romanian deadlift, and lying triceps extension finds the skull crusher. When a result came from one of those names, it says so underneath, like "Matches: RDL", so you can see why it turned up.\n\nCase, accents, spaces and hyphens don\'t matter, so "stiffleg" and "stiff-leg" are the same search. A short search won\'t match inside a word, though, so "rdl" doesn\'t pull up anything with "hurdle" in it.\n\nFree on the App Store.',
  'wd-b6-3':
    "A plan is only useful until the day doesn't go to plan. The Today screen has Fit my day for that.\n\nYou pick how long you've got, 15, 30 or 45 minutes, say you're tired or have no equipment, or swap a move you can't do for one that works the same muscles and keeps clear of any body limits you've set. It shows the change as it makes it, like \"45 → 25 min · 6 → 4 moves\", and marks every move it changed.\n\nIt's for today only. Nothing becomes a setting, and tomorrow starts from your plan again.\n\nFree on the App Store.",
}
